WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.327 --> 00:00:02.160
SPEAKER: Once your ML
model has been trained

00:00:02.160 --> 00:00:03.690
and is ready to
make predictions,

00:00:03.690 --> 00:00:05.250
you'll need to be
able to serve it.

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:09.090
Kubeflow Serving or KFServing
is a powerful open source tool

00:00:09.090 --> 00:00:11.370
you can use to serve your
models with the scale

00:00:11.370 --> 00:00:13.220
and performance of Kubernetes.

00:00:13.220 --> 00:00:14.790
Let's look at how
KFServing gives you

00:00:14.790 --> 00:00:18.027
an easy-to-use interface so you
can serve models in production.

00:00:21.665 --> 00:00:23.540
To see the documentation
on KFServing,

00:00:23.540 --> 00:00:24.840
check out the link below.

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:26.750
Kubernetes brings
powerful features

00:00:26.750 --> 00:00:28.970
to training and
serving your ML models.

00:00:28.970 --> 00:00:31.730
But it can be tricky to handle
the configuration of servers,

00:00:31.730 --> 00:00:34.520
auto scaling, networking, and
health checks of Kubernetes

00:00:34.520 --> 00:00:37.160
clusters to run your
models in production.

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:39.650
KFServing aims to
hide this complexity

00:00:39.650 --> 00:00:42.350
to bring you a simple,
pluggable, and complete story

00:00:42.350 --> 00:00:44.210
for our production ML serving.

00:00:44.210 --> 00:00:46.430
This includes everything
from prediction

00:00:46.430 --> 00:00:48.380
to pre-processing,
post-processing,

00:00:48.380 --> 00:00:50.180
and explainability.

00:00:50.180 --> 00:00:53.000
It gives you standard interfaces
for common ML frameworks

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:56.810
like TensorFlow, XGBoost,
Scikit Learn, and PyTorch,

00:00:56.810 --> 00:00:58.520
giving you a
consistent experience

00:00:58.520 --> 00:01:00.680
for models of all kinds.

00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:03.170
Its interface is a YAML
file, typically checked

00:01:03.170 --> 00:01:04.610
into a Git repository.

00:01:04.610 --> 00:01:07.130
Simply point your
specification, or spec,

00:01:07.130 --> 00:01:09.740
to a serialized model
file in cloud storage,

00:01:09.740 --> 00:01:14.070
and you get a live model
at an HTTP end point.

00:01:14.070 --> 00:01:15.870
On top of that,
KFServing lets you

00:01:15.870 --> 00:01:18.060
leverage cutting-edge
production serving features,

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:20.940
like using GPUs, auto
scaling, scaling to zero,

00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:23.580
and Canary rollouts to
your ML deployments.

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:25.650
KFServing is
installed by default

00:01:25.650 --> 00:01:27.640
as part of a Chipotle
installation,

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:30.000
but you can also install
it as a standalone using

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:31.110
a quick script.

00:01:31.110 --> 00:01:32.730
Check out the link
below to learn how.

00:01:32.730 --> 00:01:34.470
Behind the scenes
KFServing is built

00:01:34.470 --> 00:01:36.750
using the extensibility
of Kubernetes

00:01:36.750 --> 00:01:38.505
and the layers that
sit on top of it.

00:01:38.505 --> 00:01:40.620
Istio is a service
mesh layer that

00:01:40.620 --> 00:01:42.150
takes care of the
load balancing,

00:01:42.150 --> 00:01:44.520
security, and monitoring
of the micro services

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:46.440
that make up a
Kubernetes cluster.

00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:49.290
It enables KFServing to handle
traffic routing and ingress

00:01:49.290 --> 00:01:50.660
to your deployed model.

00:01:50.660 --> 00:01:53.430
Knative is a layer that
lets you deploy and manage

00:01:53.430 --> 00:01:55.370
serverless workloads
on Kubernetes.

00:01:55.370 --> 00:01:58.930
It enables KFServing to handle
networking and Canary rollouts

00:01:58.930 --> 00:02:00.930
so you can safely make
changes to your end point

00:02:00.930 --> 00:02:02.790
without dropping any traffic.

00:02:02.790 --> 00:02:06.210
And it even enables you to
auto scale GPUs and TPUs.

00:02:06.210 --> 00:02:08.580
For ML scientists, this
means adding something

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:12.270
to the KFServing resource is as
simple as using the KFServing

00:02:12.270 --> 00:02:14.800
interface that's
provided first class.

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:18.060
For example, when you want
to tweak min-max replicas

00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:20.070
or perform a canary test.

00:02:20.070 --> 00:02:22.530
For ML ops engineers, this
means applying policies

00:02:22.530 --> 00:02:24.900
under the hood becomes
much more manageable

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:26.910
with the layered
Kubernetes architecture,

00:02:26.910 --> 00:02:29.340
like when you want to apply
security policies, failure,

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:31.740
recovery metrics,
and monitoring.

00:02:31.740 --> 00:02:34.010
Let's walk through
an example interface.

00:02:34.010 --> 00:02:36.150
While you can create
a short YAML file

00:02:36.150 --> 00:02:38.760
to quickly serve your
model and get an end point,

00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:40.680
there are many ways you
can customize things

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:43.160
like authentication
and Canary rollouts.

00:02:43.160 --> 00:02:45.600
Here the min and
max specs are a way

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:48.180
to control your provisioning
of back end pods

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:50.430
to make sure that you
don't drop requests.

00:02:50.430 --> 00:02:52.770
And you can meet demand
without overspending.

00:02:52.770 --> 00:02:55.830
Behind the scenes, Knative
handles routing and queuing

00:02:55.830 --> 00:02:56.950
of traffic.

00:02:56.950 --> 00:03:00.450
You can also specify resource
requests like selecting GPUs.

00:03:00.450 --> 00:03:03.480
And thanks to Knative's
concurrency-based auto scalar,

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:06.330
you can effectively
scale CPUs or GPUs.

00:03:06.330 --> 00:03:10.240
By default, KFServing sets
target concurrency to 1.

00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:13.050
So if you load the service
with five concurrent requests,

00:03:13.050 --> 00:03:16.530
the auto scalar will scale up to
five pods, and back down to one

00:03:16.530 --> 00:03:18.670
when requests die down.

00:03:18.670 --> 00:03:21.490
For most ML scientists, one
of the biggest challenges

00:03:21.490 --> 00:03:23.050
is how to deploy
models in production

00:03:23.050 --> 00:03:24.730
using Canary rollouts.

00:03:24.730 --> 00:03:27.040
You want to be able to
easily test and update

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:30.333
your data models to production
without impacting users.

00:03:30.333 --> 00:03:32.500
While traffic splitting to
back ends is a challenge,

00:03:32.500 --> 00:03:34.900
KFServing lets you
slowly increment traffic

00:03:34.900 --> 00:03:38.660
from an old model to
an experimental model.

00:03:38.660 --> 00:03:40.220
In this example,
we're rolling out

00:03:40.220 --> 00:03:41.840
a new version of an IRIS model.

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:45.080
As we slowly increment traffic
percentage to our B2 model,

00:03:45.080 --> 00:03:46.730
we can be confident
that Canary isn't

00:03:46.730 --> 00:03:48.200
going to disrupt our users.

00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:51.500
Eventually, we'll move 100%
of traffic to our V2 model,

00:03:51.500 --> 00:03:53.270
then delete the
original deployment,

00:03:53.270 --> 00:03:56.090
allowing you to completely
manage the lifecycle of data

00:03:56.090 --> 00:03:58.710
models in production.

00:03:58.710 --> 00:04:00.540
When it comes to
processing data,

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:02.400
ML frameworks don't
give you a place

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:04.920
to execute code that
processes requests.

00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:08.130
KFServing includes hooks so you
can provide your own container

00:04:08.130 --> 00:04:11.340
implementations to do
pre and post processing.

00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:14.100
You just need to add the hook
in your KFServing interface,

00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:17.370
specify a container image spec
for your processing handler,

00:04:17.370 --> 00:04:20.100
and KFServing
automatically wires it up.

00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:21.870
The hook called
a transformer can

00:04:21.870 --> 00:04:25.530
help you process requests, like
if you want to translate a user

00:04:25.530 --> 00:04:28.740
ID into a set of new
features about the user.

00:04:28.740 --> 00:04:31.740
These new features can then be
fed into another hook called

00:04:31.740 --> 00:04:33.960
an explainer that can
perturb the features

00:04:33.960 --> 00:04:36.000
and send prediction
requests to them.

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:39.000
Perturbing features to see
its effect on a model's output

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:42.790
is one way of explaining or
shedding light on your models.

00:04:42.790 --> 00:04:45.780
Finally, the predictor can do
predictions on those requests,

00:04:45.780 --> 00:04:47.730
post process them
in the transformer,

00:04:47.730 --> 00:04:50.550
and send the transformer
requests back to you.

00:04:50.550 --> 00:04:52.530
Together, these hooks
give you a simple way

00:04:52.530 --> 00:04:55.590
to serve an end-to-end ML
model with explainability

00:04:55.590 --> 00:04:57.260
at your fingertips.

00:04:57.260 --> 00:05:00.000
KFServing gives you
out-of-the-box standardized

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:02.075
model serving across frameworks.

00:05:02.075 --> 00:05:04.200
It's built for production
inferencing with features

00:05:04.200 --> 00:05:07.410
like canarying, auto
scaling, explainability,

00:05:07.410 --> 00:05:09.330
and a clean interface,
making it easy

00:05:09.330 --> 00:05:12.030
for you to review models and
change config on the fly.

00:05:12.030 --> 00:05:14.780
[MUSIC PLAYING]

